#
# -------------------------
# To test octree-ggg-omp searching method
# -------------------------
# In Makefile_settings set:
#
# Select dimension of the run: 2, 3
# 	DEFDIMENSION = 3
#
# See other settings in Makefile_settings file.
# Then in terminal do "make clean; make all"
#
# To test performance 3pcf vs 2pcf only test TPCF = 1/0 in addons/Makefile_addons_settings
#
# Run it as:
# cd tests
# then, with the version parameter file:
# time ../cballs ./In/parameters_explained
#
# This file retains the legacy octree-ggg-omp example values below. The compact
# scalar two-ball engines are octree-2balls-omp, balltree-2balls-omp and
# kdtree-2balls-omp. Select one by changing searchMethod or overriding it:
# time ../cballs ./In/parameters_explained searchMethod=octree-2balls-omp
# Check which engines were compiled with: ../cballs options=print-search-methods
#
# -------------------------
# -------------------------
#
# Set parameters to values according to your needs.
# 	It is not necessary to give all of them explicitly. You may set only the ones
# 	needed to be set, the others will have their defaults values that are ok with you.
#
searchMethod                       = octree-ggg-omp
#
# Parameters (next three) useful to change cBalls performance
# theta controls geometric acceptance; it does not change stored coordinates
# or allocated node capacity. Larger positive values can accept coarser cell
# interactions. It is not a bound on the relative correlation error.
theta                              = 1		# increases or decreases artificially size of the cells
								# values > 1 decreases the size
								# values < 1 increases the size
nsmooth                            = 8		# in kd-tree methods act as the usual nbucket 
								# 	as it is used in other codes... set it to 8
# In compact scalar two-ball engines, nsmooth also supplies the bucket-leaf
# capacity or its base value. The effective leaf policy depends on the engine
# and statistic; some pair paths choose it adaptively. no-smooth-pivot disables
# field/pivot smoothing but does not force nsmooth to 1 or disable bucket leaves.
# Larger leaves can reduce node count while increasing work inside each leaf.
# Keep nsmooth fixed when comparing memory allocations before/after a rebuild.
rsmooth						= 
#
options                            = compute-HistN,and-CF,out-m-HistZeta	
								# you may add 'pre-/post-processing' and 
								# 	invoque the appropriate script 
								# 	in 'preScript'/'posScript' option below
# no-one-ball disables one-ball aggregation. For a scalar two-ball exact
# reference, use the full set of controls described below.
#
# -------------------------
# Compact scalar two-ball engines: traversal and memory (October 2026)
# -------------------------
# The following native tokens belong in the comma-separated options value;
# they are not additional parameter names or Python-style --flags.
#   no-smooth-pivot: use unsmoothed scalar fields/pivots.
#   scalar-pivot-reuse: enable hierarchical scalar 3PCF moment reuse on the
#     octree, PCA balltree and KD two-ball engines. This is optional.
#   dual-node-bin-theta: enable the bin-aware geometric acceptance policy.
#     This affects approximation/speed, not node-array sizing.
#
# For an exact body-level reference with these scalar two-ball engines, use
# theta=0 and options containing no-one-ball,no-two-balls,no-smooth-pivot;
# omit scalar-pivot-reuse. no-one-ball alone is not a general exact reference
# for two-ball engines. Keep the input, weights, bins and multipoles identical.
#
# CBALLS_SCALAR_PIVOT_TOL and CBALLS_SCALAR_BIN_THETA are shell environment
# variables for scalar reuse, not entries in this native parameter file.
# Example shell prefix: env CBALLS_SCALAR_PIVOT_TOL=3 CBALLS_SCALAR_BIN_THETA=0.5
# These are approximation controls, not relative-error percentages. The
# CBALLS_SHEAR_* variables control shear and do not configure scalar kappa.
# Read recorded run settings for the effective compiled/environment defaults.
#
# Smaller tree allocations are AUTOMATIC after rebuilding cballs and the
# matching cyballs extension. No option enables them. For scalar two-ball
# builders:
#   - Octree binary views count the required leaves and allocate 2*leaves-1
#     nodes. The point-buffer bound is independent of node capacity.
#   - PCA balltrees allocate exactly the nodes required by their split shape.
#   - Serial KD construction also uses exact sizing. Parallel KD already did;
#     its sparse O(N) node-count lookup is replaced by a bounded shape cache.
# The existing tree splits, leaf policy, point order, field arithmetic,
# smoothing and approximation controls are preserved. Singleton leaves still
# need 2*N-1 nodes, where N is the number of points selected for that tree.
# The octree skips the counting prepass for singleton leaves. Shear-specific
# PCA and KD builders already used exact node sizing.
#
# Smaller node reservation is not the same as a proportional decrease in
# process resident memory (RSS): unused calloc pages may never occupy RAM.
# Native bodies/cells, packed points, catalog buffers and worker scratch still
# require memory. This allocation change introduces no extra approximation;
# it does not establish a speedup or a percentage reduction in full-run RAM.
# The Python pre-load resource planner retains its conservative node estimate.
#
# Cache controls, where the selected traversal supports caching:
#   no-native-tree-cache: disable retained native-octree compact trees in the
#     scalar pair path.
#   no-balltree-tree-cache: disable retained PCA balltree caching.
# Disabling retention can reduce memory kept between calculations, but the
# active traversal still needs its tree and repeated runs must rebuild it.
# These cache options are independent of automatic exact node allocation.
#
# -------------------------
# Accuracy, timing and binning interpretation
# -------------------------
# A 5% or 10% limit on EVERY nonzero coefficient requires the maximum of
# abs(fast-exact)/abs(exact) <= 0.05 or 0.10, without a denominator floor.
# Use complex magnitudes for complex multipoles. Exact-zero references must
# stay zero; nonfinite/unsupported bins must be reported explicitly.
# Relative L2 and symmetric differences are different metrics. Python
# --qualification-rtol is an L2 target, not an every-coefficient bound.
# Match catalog selection, seed, weights, centering, geometry, bins, multipoles
# and correction policy. Two approximate engines agreeing does not establish
# exact error. A no-exact benchmark alone has no exact baseline.
#
# Profile scalar two-ball searches with options=no-out-Hist,dual-node-profile
# in a separate diagnostic run. Remove profiling for repeated timing trials
# on an otherwise idle machine. CPU sums thread work; wall is elapsed time.
# Keep loading, Python Run and native MainLoop timing scopes separate.
# The compact scalar single-neighbor multipole-product shortcut is automatic
# in rebuilt binaries; it does not add an approximation or require a new token.
#
# Angular min/max separations are outer bin EDGES, not first/last centers.
# 20 bins with edges 8--200 arcmin have centers about 8.67036--184.53294.
# The Sofia figure-1 grid has centers 8--200 arcmin and edges about
# 7.35028--217.68454 arcmin. A plotting selection cannot rebin saved data.
# Where calculation presets exist, explicit limits override preset defaults;
# plotting-only presets do not change computation bins. Check frontend help.
# See tests/python/README_run_controls.md for exact references, benchmark
# error columns, memory controls, timing scope and plotting examples.
#
# -------------------------
# Python FITS driver controls (NOT native parameter-file entries)
# -------------------------
# For python/kappa_corr_all_engines.py (tests/python/ in the source checkout):
#   --fits-chunk-pixels 1048576 bounds field-gather and coordinate-conversion
#     temporaries. Smaller chunks lower this temporary workspace, with possible
#     overhead. Selected points, their order and centering are unchanged.
#     Selection buffers are released early; final catalog/tree arrays remain.
#   --transfer-catalog-ownership explicitly avoids the Python registration
#     copy. Default registration still copies. Requires the rebuilt binding.
#     Native particle and tree storage are separate and remain allocated.
#   Python API equivalent: balls.set_catalog(positions, kappa=kappa,
#     take_ownership=True). Supplied arrays must satisfy the binding's strict
#     ownership/dtype/layout requirements. They become read-only; callers must
#     stop mutating/resizing all aliases. See the driver README for the contract.
#   --nside-down 0 retains native map resolution; a lower positive NSIDE
#     changes map resolution. --max-points 0 retains all selected points;
#     a positive limit thins the catalog, which is different from cell smoothing.
#   --dual-node-bin-theta adds the native dual-node-bin-theta options token.
#     --more-options scalar-pivot-reuse,no-smooth-pivot passes native tokens.
# Chunking, ownership transfer and smaller node allocation preserve the input
# sample and accuracy settings. Downsampling, thinning and looser acceptance
# controls require a separate accuracy comparison on matching inputs/settings.
# Reducing numberThreads can also reduce per-worker scratch at a speed cost.
# See tests/python/README_kappa_corr_all_engines.md for the full driver options.
# 
# Give the name of the point catalog to analyze and its format.
infile                             = ./catalogs/Abraham/kappa_nres12_zs9NS256r000.bin
infileformat                       = binary
# can be columns-ascii (default value), binary or takahasi
iCatalogs                          = 1
# this is the tag associated to catalog giving in above
								# several files can be given, the 'iCatalog' tag them
								# this is useful to do cross-correlations
#
rootDir                            = Output
#
# Set of parameters to define histogram bins
rangeN                             = 0.0633205
rminHist                           = 0.00213811
sizeHistN                          = 20
#
# Give the number of Chebyshev multipoles to compute. Is active when cBalls has been given computeTPCF = true
mChebyshev                         = 7		# mChebyshev + 1 must be a power of 2
stepState                          = 10000		# gives the frequency to save the the number of pivot computed. Useful to see the state of the run
#
verbose                            = 2
verbose_log                        = 2
#
# Give the number of threads to use if cBalls has been configured with OPENMPMACHINE = 1
numberThreads                      = 16
#
#
# -------------------------
# Nothing to change below
# -------------------------
#
# You may save the input catalog in a different format:
outfile                            =
# if it empty no file is save
									# if it is not empty file is saved
									#	but use in combination with
									#	'options = stop' because normally
									#	just want to save the file in a
									#	different format or check if data 
									#	read properly
									# 'options = stop' means cBalls do not
									#	follow the searching process
outfileformat                      = columns-ascii
#
# To compute 3pcf in harmonic base set to true. Else false
#computeTPCF                         = true
# For histograms in logscale version set to true. Else false
useLogHist                          = true
# And set the number of bins per decade:
logHistBinsPD                       = 5
#
# To use periodic boundary conditions set it true.
usePeriodic                         = false
#
# Set of 4 parameters to set a region on the unit sphere.
#	These parameters act only when reading a Takahashi simulation.
thetaL                             = 1.27993
thetaR                             = 1.86166
phiL                               = 1.28011
phiR                               = 1.86187
#
# Set of parameters to define names of the histograms output files
histNNFileName                      = histNN
histXi2pcfFileName                 = histXi2pcf
histZetaFileName                  = histZetaM
#mhistZetaFileName                  = mhistZeta
suffixOutFiles
#
# Python/shell/C script to use as a pre/post processing
#	in options above add 'pre-processing' and/or 'post-processing'
preScript                             = ""
posScript                             = ""
# you can set, i.e., "python scripts/plot2pcf.py"
									#	in order to plot the 2pcf
									#	at the end of the computation
									#	if use with 'options = post-processing'
#
# Set of parameters to define a test model, computed inside the code.
# They will be useful to compute random catalogs to build estimators.
testmodel                          = unit-sphere-random
nbody                              = 262144
lengthBox                          = 2
seed                               = 123			# to init random number generator
#
# For the simple-cubic-random test model use the following values for above parameters: 
# sizeHistN                        = 40
# rangeN				      = 100.0
# lengthBox                          = 512
#

% Frontend memory controls (2026-10-17):
% --fits-chunk-pixels applies to kappa/shear HEALPix drivers and benchmarks.
% --transfer-catalog-ownership is opt-in for kappa/shear/Ly-alpha Python
% registration, including private *_dual_node.py native paths. Default: copy.
% Forest IDs still need private compaction; project delta before transfer.
% Executable/file benchmarks (ENCORE) have no NumPy transfer setting.
% These are Python CLI flags, not parameters or options= tokens.
% Smaller compact scalar octree/PCA/KD allocations activate automatically
% after rebuilding; selection, leaf policy and approximation are unchanged.
% See tests/python/README_memory_controls.md for ownership, RSS and timing.
